'''Tinnsjå'''<ref name="name">{{Cite web |title=Informasjon om stadnamn |url=https://stadnamn.kartverket.no/fakta/3008 |access-date=2026-08-22 |website=Norgeskart |publisher=[[Norwegian Mapping Authority|Kartverket]] |language=no}}</ref> ({{lit|Lake Tinn}}<ref>{{Cite book |last=Sjåvik |first=Jan |title=The A to Z of Norway |publisher=Scarecrow Press |year=2010 |location=Lanham, MD |page=233}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Gallagher |first=Thomas |title=Assault in Norway: Sabotaging The Nazi Nuclear Program |publisher=Lyons Press |year=2010 |location=Guilford, CT |page=173}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Mead |first=William Richard |title=How People Live in Norway |publisher=Ward Lock Educational Co. Ltd. |year=1965 |location=London |page=43}}</ref>) is one of the largest [[List of Norwegian Lakes|lakes]] in [[Norway]] measuring about {{convert|51.38|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}}. At a depth of {{convert|460|m|ft|abbr=on}} it is the third [[List of lakes by depth|deepest]] lake in Norway and Europe. Tinnsjå is located in [[Tinn Municipality]] and [[Notodden Municipality]] in [[Telemark]] county. At its source in the west, the [[Måna]] river flows out of the lake [[Møsvatn]] and past the town of [[Rjukan]] into Tinnsjå. From the north, the river [[Mår (river)|Mår]] flows from the lakes [[Mår (lake)|Mår]], [[Gøystavatn]], and [[Kalhovdfjorden]] into Tinnsjå. Tinnsjå is part of the [[Skien watershed]], and it drains via the [[Tinnåa]] river in the south, down to the lake [[Heddalsvatnet]].<ref name="snl">{{Cite SNL|Tinnsjå|Tinnsjå|2026-08-22|Lundbo|Sten|2026-05-11|editor2-first=Lars|editor2-last=Mæhlum}}</ref>

At the north end of the lake lie the villages of [[Atrå]] and [[Austbygdi, Telemark|Austbygdi]]. The village of [[Miland]] lies on the western shore of the lake. The village of [[Hovin, Tinn|Hovin]] lies up on a hill overlooking the eastern shore of the lake and the village of [[Rudsgrendi]] lies on the western shore. There is a small [[dam]] at the south end of the lake which regulates the surface elevation of the lake and the village of [[Tinnoset]] is located at this end of the lake. The village of [[Gransherad (village)|Gransherad]] lies about {{convert|4|km|mi|abbr=on}} south of this dam.<ref name="snl" />

==History==
In 1944, during the German occupation of Norway, the [[Tinnsjø railway ferry|ferry]] [[SF Hydro|SF ''Hydro'']] [[Norwegian heavy water sabotage|was sunk in Tinnsjå by the Norwegian resistance]]. The Germans were using the ferry to transport a large quantity of [[heavy water]] to [[Germany]], where it was to be used for [[nuclear weapons research]]. The heavy water had been produced at [[Vemork]], a factory located in [[Rjukan]]. 

The wreck of the ferry was discovered in 1993.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Payton |first=Gary |title=Rjukanbanen; på sporet av et industrieventyr |last2=Lepperød |first2=Trond |publisher=Mana Forlag |year=1995 |isbn=8299354919 |location=Rjukan |language=Norwegian}}</ref> In 2004, it was investigated and filmed for an episode of [[Nova (American TV program)|NOVA]]; heavy water samples were recovered and [[deuterium]] [[Isotope separation|isotopic enrichment]] was confirmed.

In 2004, a film crew shooting footage for a new documentary on the heavy water sabotage became aware of an unusual fish, swimming near the lake bottom at a depth of {{convert|430|m}}. Two specimens of the previously unknown fish were captured in April 2005. Analysis revealed the fish to be closely related to [[Arctic char]]. The light-colored, translucent fish is up to {{convert|15|cm}} long and lacks a [[swim bladder]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mathismoen |first=Ole |date=2005-05-11 |title=Ny fisk oppdaget |trans-title=New Fish Discovered |url=https://www.aftenposten.no/norge/i/LlX54/ny-fisk-oppdaget |access-date=2015-02-01 |work=Aftenposten}}</ref>

